  "description": "Filter DataFrame rows by evaluating a numerical column condition threshold, preserving column order.\n\nBoolean indexing is the foundational filtering idiom in pandas for row selection.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{df}_{\\text{filtered}} = \\text{df}[\\text{df}[\\text{filter\\_col}] > \\tau]\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Construct boolean series predicate on target column\n2. Index DataFrame to extract conforming rows\n3. Serialize resulting DataFrame to records list\n\n### Returns\nList of records passing the threshold criterion.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Boolean Indexing\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Boolean Indexing** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nBoolean indexing is the foundational filtering idiom in pandas for row selection. In this problem, the goal is to: filter dataframe rows by evaluating a numerical column condition threshold, preserving column order.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{df}_{\\text{filtered}} = \\text{df}[\\text{df}[\\text{filter\\_col}] > \\tau]\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Construct boolean series predicate on target column\n2. **Phase 2**: Index DataFrame to extract conforming rows\n3. **Phase 3**: Serialize resulting DataFrame to records list\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"name\": \"A\", \"score\": 75}, {\"name\": \"B\", \"score\": 90}, {\"name\": \"C\", \"score\": 85}], filter_col = \"score\", threshold = 80.0\n\nOutput: [{\"name\": \"B\", \"score\": 90}, {\"name\": \"C\", \"score\": 85}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_boolean_indexing under standard test conditions."
  ],
